Q: Difference between x.toString() and x+""
A: <p>In C# I would completely disregard this advice in favor of something more declarative such as using <code>.ToString</code> directly.  </p>

<p>The only potential advantage this syntax provides you is it will create an empty string if <code>x</code> is <code>null</code>.  I would favor an extension method if this is considered an advantage in your code.  </p>

<pre><code>public static string NullSafeToString&lt;T&gt;(this T value) where T : class {
  return value == null ? "" : value.ToString();
}
</code></pre>

<p>The other reason to avoid this is because it can create precedence confusion.  For example do you know exactly what the value of <code>z</code> is in the following scenario without the use of reference materials?  </p>

<pre><code>int x = 42;
int y = 15;
string z = x + y + "";
</code></pre>

<p>Now same question with the following</p>

<pre><code>int x = 42;
int y = 15;
string z = x + y.ToString();
</code></pre>

<p>The latter is more likely to be understood at a glance by the average developer who hasn't taken the time to memory C# operator precedence.  Hence I would prefer it because it has a lesser chance of being misunderstood. </p>

